<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>hello-world</displayName>
  <description>Settings for the hello-world daily program.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="HelloWorld">hello-world</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_23">hello-world 1.23.0 or later</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_24">hello-world 1.24.0 or later</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_25">hello-world 1.25.0 or later</string>
      <string id="DisableSignInLauncher">Turn off opening hello-world at sign-in</string>
      <string id="DisableSignInLauncher_Help">When enabled, people can't set hello-world to open by itself when they sign in, it never offers to, and a launcher they turned on earlier is removed the next time they open the program.

When disabled or not configured, each person can turn the sign-in opening on or off with menu option 2.

The launcher is a value named hello-world under HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run.</string>
      <string id="HideThoughtAndTip">Hide the daily thought and tip</string>
      <string id="HideThoughtAndTip_Help">When enabled, hello-world doesn't show the thought for today or the tip to try. The plan question and the menu still work.

When disabled or not configured, each person can hide or show them with menu option 8.</string>
      <string id="HideDaysInARow">Hide the days-in-a-row message</string>
      <string id="HideDaysInARow_Help">When enabled, hello-world never shows the message that counts how many days in a row someone opened it.

When disabled or not configured, the message shows only on the 3rd, 7th and 14th day in a row and every 30th after that, and each person can hide it with menu option 3.</string>
      <string id="DisablePlans">Turn off plans</string>
      <string id="DisablePlans_Help">When enabled, hello-world doesn't ask for a plan or follow one up, and keeps no plan text, finished plans or count of plans done. Plan text already saved is dropped the next time each person opens the program. People still see the greeting and, unless that is also hidden, the thought and tip.

Use this where plan text typed into a local file would be a records or works-council concern.

When disabled or not configured, people can type one optional plan a day.</string>
      <string id="ForceEnglish">Always show hello-world in English</string>
      <string id="ForceEnglish_Help">When enabled, hello-world shows its screens in English for everyone.

When disabled or not configured, hello-world shows its screens in Spanish, French, Portuguese or German when the Windows display language is one of those, and in English otherwise. The command words of every language, such as hecho and quitter, work in every language.

An organization content file replaces the thoughts and tips in every language.</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_28">hello-world 1.28.0 or later</string>
      <string id="UseTextScreen">Open hello-world as a text screen</string>
      <string id="UseTextScreen_Help">When enabled, the Start menu shortcut opens hello-world as a text screen in a console window, as versions before 1.28 did, and the sign-in launcher opens it there too. Nobody can switch to the window.

When disabled or not configured, hello-world opens in a window with buttons, and each person can switch to the text screen under Options, or with option 9 in the text screen's menu. With the sign-in launcher on, the window version shows a Windows notification at sign-in when there is a plan to ask about, instead of opening.

Use this setting where screen reader users have tested only the text screen, or where Windows notifications are not wanted.</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_31">hello-world 1.31.0 or later</string>
      <string id="TurnOnReminder">Turn on the sign-in reminder for everyone</string>
      <string id="TurnOnReminder_Help">When enabled, hello-world turns on its reminder for each person the next time they open it, unless they already answered the reminder question. With the window, the reminder is a Windows notification at sign-in when there is a plan to ask about; with the text screen, hello-world opens once a day at sign-in. Each person can still turn it off under Options or with menu option 2, and it stays off.

When disabled or not configured, hello-world asks each person once.

"Turn off opening hello-world at sign-in" wins over this setting.</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_1_32">hello-world 1.32.0 or later</string>
      <string id="ReportUsage">Write usage events to the event log</string>
      <string id="ReportUsage_Help">When enabled, hello-world writes one event to the Application event log, source hello-world, event 2000, each time someone opens it, sets a plan or finishes one: "hello-world: opened", "hello-world: plan set" or "hello-world: plan finished". The events hold no plan text, and nothing is sent over a network; your own event collection decides where they go.

When disabled or not configured, hello-world writes no usage events.</string>
      <string id="LogErrors">Keep an error log</string>
      <string id="LogErrors_Help">When enabled, hello-world adds a line to errors.log in each person's hello-world data folder when something goes wrong: the time, the version, the error's type and the lines of hello.py involved. It holds no plan text and stays under 100 KB.

When disabled or not configured, hello-world keeps no error log.</string>
      <string id="TimestampBackups">Name backups of a damaged file by date and time</string>
      <string id="TimestampBackups_Help">When enabled, a damaged notes.json is set aside as notes.json.&lt;date-time&gt;.bak instead of notes.json.bak, notes.json.bak2 and so on.

When disabled or not configured, backups are numbered.</string>
      <string id="LeaveDamagedFile">Leave a damaged file alone when nobody is at the screen</string>
      <string id="LeaveDamagedFile_Help">When enabled, a run with nobody at the keyboard, such as a sign-in run with no console, leaves a damaged notes.json where it is instead of setting it aside. The next visit with someone there sets it aside and says so.

When disabled or not configured, a damaged file is set aside as a backup whenever it is found.</string>
      <string id="MaxBackups">Keep at most this many backups of a damaged file</string>
      <string id="MaxBackups_Help">When enabled, hello-world deletes the oldest backups of a damaged notes.json beyond this number before it makes a new one.

When disabled or not configured, every backup is kept until the person deletes everything.</string>
      <string id="FeedbackAddress">Address for feedback</string>
      <string id="FeedbackAddress_Help">When enabled, the window's Options menu has "Send feedback...", which opens a new mail to this address in the person's own mail program. hello-world itself sends nothing.

When disabled or not configured, there is no feedback item.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="FeedbackAddress">
        <textBox refId="FeedbackAddress"><label>Address:</label></textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="MaxBackups">
        <decimalTextBox refId="MaxBackups" defaultValue="5">Backups to keep:</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
